  "note": "TWO expansions differenced by Join{anti} on node identity: Expand(KNOWS, both, bounded(1, $nearMaxHops)) is the near set, Expand(KNOWS, both, bounded(1, $maxPathDistance)) the far set, and the candidate set is far minus near. **The difference construction is REQUIRED, not an optimization** (FREEZE §7.5, and tgms/tgir/eval/expand.py's `_node_set` says the same): §2.3 deduplicates a `bounded(a,b)` at the minimum j WITHIN [a,b], so a single `bounded($min,$max)` would admit a node whose true minimum distance is below $min — it is not a min-distance band. Then exact(1) IS_LOCATED_IN / IS_PART_OF with a PropertyPredicate on the country, HAS_CREATOR in -> message, a semi-join against the DISTINCT set of messages whose Tag has $tagClass (§2.8), HAS_TAG out -> tag, Aggregate(group_by=[candidate, tag], count_distinct(message)), Order, Limit(100).",
  "implementation_note": "R5 makes both apoc bounds bind-time known (§4.3), so `$nearMaxHops` is $minPathDistance - 1 resolved at bind time; with $minPathDistance = 2 it is 1. The Join{inner} semi-join takes Project/Aggregate inputs because `eval_join`'s inner branch asserts the realized schema equals the declared one, which a column-pruned scan never satisfies (tgms/tgir/eval/join.py:65)."
 },
